import json
Q=[]
def E(n,cat,q,o,a,hint,exp,tag='r'):
    L="ABCDEF"
    Q.append({"id":f"s1-Q{n}","set":"set1","question":q,"options":{L[i]:t for i,t in enumerate(o)},"answers":list(a),"category":cat,"source":"ikm-set1","v4":tag,"hint":hint,"explanation":exp})
E(1,"Change Enablement","Which of the following are typically recorded in a change record (request for change, RFC) for change enablement?",["Review date","Reason for change","A copy of the whole change process","Details of how the change will be implemented","The priority of the change"],"ABDE","Think about what an approver needs for THIS change: why, how, when to review it and how urgent it is.","**Correct: A, B, D and E.** A change record captures the specifics of one change. A copy of the generic change process is not change-specific, and the source also marked C as wrong. *V4 note:* ITIL 4 (change enablement) does not prescribe fixed RFC fields; these are the commonly recorded ones, same as in ITIL v3.")
E(2,"Knowledge Management","Which of the following measures show that knowledge is available and helping to support decisions and resolution?",["Increased number of changes rejected by the change authority","Increased percentage of incidents solved using known errors and knowledge articles","Decreased use of knowledge articles, regardless of quality","Increased scores in regular satisfaction surveys on knowledge management","Increased number of accesses to the knowledge base by managers and staff"],"BDE","Look for measures that show knowledge being USED and valued.","**Correct: B, D and E.** Rising reuse of known errors/articles, better satisfaction and more access all show knowledge is available and useful. A is a change measure and C is the opposite of success. *V4 note:* v3 said SKMS; ITIL 4 uses the knowledge management practice and its knowledge base. Options A and C were replaced because they relied on v3 CMDB/review-date wording.")
E(3,"Capacity & Performance","Which of the following are NOT typical measures for capacity and performance management?",["Accuracy of capacity forecasts versus actual demand","Percentage of leave requests submitted on time","Percentage of services with performance monitoring in place","Number of incidents caused by capacity shortages","Percentage of changes approved by the change authority"],"BE","Capacity and performance management is about making sure resources meet current and future demand.","**Correct: B and E.** A, C and D directly measure forecasting, monitoring and capacity-related failures. Leave requests (B) and change approval rates (E) belong elsewhere. *V4 note:* rewritten for ITIL 4 'capacity and performance management'; the v3 list (e.g. 'suitable underpinning contracts') mixed in supplier management and could not be verified.")
E(4,"Service Level Management","Which of the following show the effectiveness of service level management?",["Percentage of service targets being met","Percentage of changes implemented on time","Accurate, up-to-date growth forecasts","Number of expedited records evaluated in change authority meetings","Percentage of services covered by service level agreements"],"AE","SLM is about agreeing service levels and meeting them.","**Correct: A and E.** Targets met and SLA coverage measure SLM directly. B and D are change measures; C is a capacity/demand measure. Unchanged in ITIL 4 (service level management).","k")
E(5,"Continual Improvement","In ITIL 4, which of the following drives ongoing improvement across the whole service value system, in the same spirit as the Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle?",["The continual improvement practice and model","Service desk","Deployment management","Change enablement","Supplier management"],"A","Look for the element whose whole job is improving everything else.","**Correct: A.** ITIL 4 provides the continual improvement model (with the 'Improve' value chain activity and the continual improvement practice). *V4 note:* the ITIL v3 answer was 'Continual Service Improvement', a lifecycle stage that no longer exists in ITIL 4.")
E(6,"Service Catalogue","Which of the following correctly describes the purpose of service catalogue management?",["Provide a single source of consistent information on all services and service offerings, available to the relevant audience","Work with configuration management to align all CIs to published services","Work with strategy and transition teams to implement all new services","Capture the maintenance and use of asset and configuration data"],"A","Think 'one authoritative list' of what the provider offers.","**Correct: A.** This is the ITIL 4 purpose of service catalogue management. B and D describe configuration/asset management; C describes implementation work.","k")
E(7,"Supplier Management","A change manager needs the basic terms of a contract with a supplier. What is the best source?",["The knowledge management database (KMDB)","The supplier and contract management information system (SCMIS) / supplier and contract register","Hold a meeting with the service level management team","Open a request with the service desk","Search the CMDB, since contracts are maintained there"],"B","Which repository is built specifically to hold supplier and contract details?","**Correct: B.** ITIL 4's supplier management practice keeps contract terms in a supplier and contract management information system. *V4 note:* ITIL v3 called this the Supplier and Contract Database (SCD).")
E(9,"Service Configuration","With which of the following must service configuration management be integrated to keep configuration information accurate and up to date?",["Change enablement","Release management","Capacity and performance management","Service continuity management","Service financial management"],"AB","Configuration records go stale when things are changed or released without updating them.","**Correct: A and B.** Changes and releases alter configuration items, so those practices must update the records. *V4 note:* v3 names (Change, Release Management) mapped to change enablement and release management.")
E(11,"Service Continuity","Which of the following is NOT an objective of service continuity management?",["Conduct regular business impact analysis so plans stay current","Conduct regular risk assessments with the business to ensure continuity","Maintain IT continuity plans so recovery mechanisms meet business expectations","Ensure proper recovery mechanisms are in place to meet business expectations","Ensure release management builds release versions based on the impact of change records"],"E","Four of these are about recovery and continuity; one is about how releases are built.","**Correct: E.** Release versioning belongs to release/change work. Options C and D are both valid continuity objectives, so only E is the odd one out. (One copy of the source also listed D, which is not correct.)")
E(12,"Release & Deployment","Which of the following are valid measures of success for release and deployment?",["A formal process exists to decide which services to provide","Appropriate data transfer is ensured, with no loss or corruption","Minimizing business impact of incidents that cannot be prevented","Increasing the number of customer surveys completed","Reduced resources and cost to diagnose and fix incidents and problems caused by releases in live use"],"BE","Look for outcomes a good release or deployment directly produces.","**Correct: B and E.** Clean data transfer and fewer live-use fixes show deployments are landing well. A relates to portfolio decisions, C to continuity/incident work, D to feedback gathering. *V4 note:* mapped to release management and deployment management.")
E(13,"Service Financial Mgmt","Which of the following are valid success factors for service financial management?",["Funding is available to support the provision of services","Relationship management provides information to financial management","The ability to establish and articulate business requirements for new or changed services","An enterprise-wide framework exists to identify, manage and communicate service information","Regular reports are produced on the costs and use of customer and service assets"],"AE","Success here means money is available and its use is visible.","**Correct: A and E.** Funding and cost/usage reporting are core financial outcomes. C is a business analysis outcome and D is knowledge management. *Note:* the source key was A, C and E; C does not belong to financial management.")
E(18,"Incident Management","Which of the following correctly describes how incoming calls to the service desk are categorized and routed?",["Requests for increased security access are forwarded to application development","Requests for increased security access are forwarded to the compliance manager","Requests to test network connectivity between two offices go to service validation and testing","Reports of an unplanned interruption to a service are forwarded to incident management","Requests for a change proposal are forwarded to service management"],"D","An unplanned interruption has a specific name in ITIL.","**Correct: D.** An unplanned interruption or degradation is an incident and is handled by incident management. Access requests are service requests; change proposals go to change enablement.","k")
E(19,"Service Continuity","Which of the following are valid critical success factors for service continuity management?",["Awareness throughout the organization of business and IT continuity plans","IT services are delivered and can be recovered to meet supplier objectives","Increase in validated awareness of business impact, needs and requirements throughout IT","Risk assessment and management are conducted in isolation","Overall reduction in the risk and impact of possible failures of IT services"],"ACE","Watch for options that name the wrong goal (suppliers) or a bad practice (isolation).","**Correct: A, C and E.** B should refer to *business* objectives, not supplier objectives, and D describes poor practice. (The two source copies disagreed: A+E vs A+C+E; C is valid.)")
E(22,"Capacity & Performance","Which of the following act as triggers for capacity and performance management?",["Ad hoc capacity and performance reports","New and changed services, and review or revision of SLAs","Supplier financial issues","User financial issues","New or changed corporate governance guidelines"],"B","Capacity work is triggered by changes to what the service must deliver.","**Correct: B.** New/changed services and SLA revisions alter the demand or targets to plan capacity for. Reports are outputs, not triggers, and the other options are unrelated to capacity.","k")
E(23,"Supplier Management","Which practice must supplier management work with to develop consistent, reliable approaches to supplier contracts and agreements?",["Capacity and performance management","Availability management","Service level management","Service continuity management","Service catalogue management"],"C","Contract terms need to line up with the service targets promised to customers.","**Correct: C.** Supplier contracts must support the service levels agreed with customers, so supplier management works with service level management.","k")
E(24,"Change Enablement","Which of the following statements about the change schedule are correct?",["It lists planned changes and their proposed implementation dates","It is always created by release management and passed to change enablement for review","It helps avoid clashes between changes and tells stakeholders what is planned","It only lists the categories of change, not individual changes","It contains only changes that will not affect service levels"],"AC","The schedule is a calendar of specific planned changes.","**Correct: A and C.** *V4 note:* v3's 'Forward Schedule of Change' and 'Projected Service Outage' are now the change schedule in change enablement. The original key (A, C, E) relied on v3-only terms and was rewritten.")
E(26,"Incident Management","Incidents appear right after a release team applied a change in production. How will support proceed?",["Notify the monitoring team to solve the incidents","Let the incidents sit in queues for the next support level","Notify the release team to back out the change","Escalate the incidents to the next level so they are resolved","Tell monitoring to change thresholds so the incidents are no longer captured"],"CD","The goal is to restore service, not hide or ignore the symptoms.","**Correct: C and D.** Backing out the suspect change and escalating restores service quickly. Passing incidents to monitoring, waiting in queues or hiding alerts does not.","k")
E(28,"Service Financial Mgmt","How does a business benefit from service financial management?",["It helps asset management with depreciation models","It ensures the IT organization follows SOX compliance processes","It provides better planning and forecasting of the money needed to cover the cost of services","Aligning infrastructure to change governance means less spending on outages","Aligning services to business expectations creates more appropriate, controllable spending and more predictable profitability"],"CE","Look for benefits about planning and controlling spend.","**Correct: C and E.** Better forecasting and controllable spending are core benefits. A, B and D belong to asset management, compliance and change governance.","k")
E(29,"Deployment","Which of the following are deployment approaches described in ITIL 4?",["Phased deployment","Big bang deployment","Temporary deployment","Pull deployment","Delta deployment"],"ABD","ITIL 4 lists four approaches; one of them is 'continuous delivery'.","**Correct: A, B and D.** ITIL 4 lists phased, big bang, continuous delivery and pull. *V4 note:* the ITIL v3 question asked for release types (package, delta, full), which ITIL 4 no longer defines, so it was rewritten.")
E(31,"Knowledge Management","Which of the following can an IT organization use to cut incident and problem resolution times through maintained, accessible knowledge records containing workarounds?",["Deployment management","Service configuration management (CMDB)","Knowledge management (knowledge base)","Change enablement","Service level management"],"C","Which practice exists to keep reusable know-how findable?","**Correct: C.** A maintained knowledge base of workarounds speeds resolution. *V4 note:* v3 answer was the SKMS.")
E(32,"Continual Improvement","Which of the following are valid measures of the continual improvement practice?",["Number of improvement opportunities identified and logged","Number of identified contract breaches","Capacity reserves","Number of major security incidents","Improvement initiatives are reviewed and tracked to completion"],"AE","Choose measures about improvement activity itself.","**Correct: A and E.** B belongs to supplier management, C to capacity, D to security. The two source copies disagreed (D vs A+E); A and E fit improvement work.")
E(33,"Availability","Which of the following is a key element of the REACTIVE activities of availability management?",["Rapidly integrating hardware technology to make the infrastructure more available","Planning, design and improvement of availability","Gathering technical requirements to make sure systems are available","Monitoring, measuring and analysis of all change records going into production","Monitoring, measuring and analysis of all events, incidents and problems around unavailability"],"E","'Reactive' means responding to what has already gone wrong.","**Correct: E only.** Reactive activities analyse events, incidents and problems involving unavailability; A-C are proactive design/improvement. The source also listed D, which is not a reactive availability activity.")
E(35,"Service Continuity","Which of the following are advantages of service continuity management?",["Staff able to decide the business continuity need","Supports the overall business continuity plan","Ability to recover in a controlled manner","Ability to recover all resources regardless of business need","Ability to control the annual spending of continuity management"],"BC","Continuity is driven by business need, not by IT preference.","**Correct: B and C.** It supports the business plan and enables controlled recovery. Recovering everything regardless of need (D) ignores priorities.","k")
E(36,"Service Configuration","Which of the following must be recorded for every configuration item (CI)?",["Version number","Unique identifier","CI type","Manufacturer information","Purchase cost"],"BC","What's the minimum needed to tell any two CIs apart and classify them?","**Correct: B and C.** Every CI needs a unique ID and a type. Versions, manufacturer and cost apply only to some CIs. *Note:* the source key (serial number and unique ID) was incorrect since not every CI has a serial number.")
E(37,"Deployment","A company will roll out a POS upgrade to all retail stores. Which actions minimize the risk to store operations?",["Divide stores into groups (e.g. by region) and deploy in phases","Deploy to every store at once during trading hours","Start with a small pilot group of stores, then widen the roll-out","Skip the back-out plan to save time","Prepare a back-out plan and train store staff for the upgrade"],"ACE","Risk falls when you go small first, and have a way back.","**Correct: A, C and E.** Phased and pilot roll-outs limit exposure; a back-out plan and trained staff limit impact. *V4 note:* rewritten around ITIL 4 deployment approaches; the source key endorsed a big-bang roll-out, which is less clearly risk-minimizing.")
E(39,"Continual Improvement","Which of the following is the purpose of the continual improvement practice?",["Ensure external suppliers meet OLAs and customers are satisfied with their services","Analyze all changes with a change impact analysis tool","Create a single technology to maintain integration across applications","Continually align IT services with changing business needs by identifying and implementing improvements","Store all infrastructure data in a configuration database"],"D","It's about staying aligned with what the business needs over time.","**Correct: D only.** A mixes supplier management with OLAs (OLAs are internal), B is change, E is configuration management. (The source key was A and D; A is not correct.)")
E(41,"Service Financial Mgmt","How does service financial management help with understanding the value of a service?",["By establishing best finance practices and policies","By working with the service desk so all support costs are covered","By working with accounting on the balance sheet","By providing means to understand a service's cost relative to its business value, using accounting methods and ROI approaches","By charging customers for use of the infrastructure"],"D","Value means comparing cost against what the business gets.","**Correct: D.** Service valuation relates cost to business value. *V4 note:* service valuation is part of service financial management in ITIL 4.","k")
E(44,"Change Enablement","Which of the following best defines change enablement?",["A group of people with authority to approve changes","A group of people who advise on implementing changes","The process to implement changes that require release management","Maximizing the number of successful changes by ensuring risks are properly assessed, authorizing changes to proceed and managing the change schedule","A record of which CIs are affected by an authorized change"],"D","The answer describes a practice's purpose, not a group or a record.","**Correct: D.** This is ITIL 4's purpose statement. A is a change authority, B a former CAB role, E a change record. *V4 note:* 'change management' is now 'change enablement'.")
E(46,"Knowledge Management","How can the knowledge management practice help the services team with their documentation?",["Work with service owners to capture information about services going into production","Approve all changes to production","Review knowledge on retired or outdated services and decide what to update or archive","Ask external vendors to review the stored data","Collect and store information about services currently in production in a knowledge base"],"ACE","Knowledge management captures, curates and shares information.","**Correct: A, C and E.** Capturing, curating and storing service information is core; approving changes and outsourcing review are not.")
E(47,"Monitoring & Event Mgmt","Which of the following events belong to event management, and how are they classified?",["A user logging in to an application is an informational event","A CI sending an automated notification when conditions are met is a filtered event","An email reaching its intended recipient is an exception event","A transaction taking 15% longer than normal is a warning event","A user entering an incorrect password is an exception event"],"ADE","Events are classified as informational, warning or exception; 'filtered' is not one of them.","**Correct: A, D and E.** Normal activity is informational, a deviation is a warning, a failure to comply is an exception. B is wrong because 'filtered' is not a classification, and C is informational. *Note:* the source key included B, which is incorrect.")
E(48,"Problem Management","Which of the following are activities in problem management?",["Problem identification","Problem control","Error control","Approving standard changes","Overseeing access to services"],"ABC","ITIL 4 splits problem management into three phases.","**Correct: A, B and C.** ITIL 4 describes problem identification, problem control and error control (which includes managing known errors). *V4 note:* replaces the v3 activity list; D and E belong to change enablement and access management.")
open("../data_set1.js","w").write("// IKM Set 1: combined, deduplicated and validated against ITIL 4.\nwindow.QUESTION_BANK = (window.QUESTION_BANK || []).concat(\n"+json.dumps(Q,indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)+"\n);\n")
print(len(Q))
